How To Incorporate Indicator Constraints in JuMP
Some solvers allow you to directly translate indicator constraints into JuMP. For example,
@variable(waste, Y[1:3], Bin)
@constraint(waste, commit2, !Y[2] =&gt; {W[1,2] + W[2,2] == 0})
defines \(Y_j\) as a binary indicator variable and implements \[Y_2 = \begin{cases}0 &\quad \text{if } W_{21} + W_{22} = 0 \\ 1 & \quad \text{else} \end{cases}
\]

